# ADR-002: Distributed Read-Only Git Storage via In-Cluster Replica, Decoupled Node FUSE Daemons, and Thin CSI

* **Status:** Accepted
* **Date:** 2026-08-27
* **Deciders:** Architecture & Platform Engineering Teams

---

## Context and Problem Statement

Apps across a multi-node Kubernetes cluster need fast, read-only access to versioned config, schemas, and asset trees from a trunk-based GitLab monorepo.

When an MR merges to `main`, those updates must show up for workloads **without** each pod cloning Git, rebuilding images, or restarting.

### What we must satisfy

* **No pull storms on GitLab.** Hundreds of nodes/pods cloning corporate GitLab at once causes rate limits, WAN cost, and saturation. We want **one** (or few) in-cluster mirror(s); nodes pull from that.
* **Unprivileged apps.** Application pods stay locked down (`runAsNonRoot`, drop all caps, no `/dev/fuse`, no `hostPath`). Privileged work stays in platform DaemonSets (`git-csi-system`).
* **Upgradeable platform without breaking reads.** Rolling the CSI driver or sync logic must not cut live file I/O in apps.
* **Stable views when needed.** Some apps boot-lock to one Git version for their lifetime; others may track live trunk.
* **Developers pick version in app config.** No annotations / CSI attributes for tag or SHA — apps use `CONFIG_PATH`.
* **Protect the node OS disk.** Git objects and caches live on **dedicated** node storage so root (`/`) does not fill and trigger `NodeHasDiskPressure`.

---

## Plain-language building blocks

These terms show up throughout the decision. The substance is technical; the analogies are only for orientation.

| Term | Plain meaning | Analogy |
| --- | --- | --- |
| **In-cluster Git replica** | A Git server **inside** the cluster that holds a copy of the monorepo | A **branch library** in the building: everyone borrows from it instead of driving across town to HQ (GitLab) |
| **FUSE** | A small privileged process that makes a **virtual folder** look like a real filesystem (reads are answered in userspace) | A **librarian at a desk**: when you open a path under `/mnt/git`, the librarian fetches the right page. If the librarian leaves, the desk is still there but reading fails |
| **CSI** | Kubernetes plug-in that attaches storage into a pod when it starts | The **building keycard system**: it does not own the library; it only unlocks “put this folder at `/mnt/git` inside this apartment (pod)” |
| **Bind-mount** | “Same folder, second door” — the pod sees the node’s FUSE export at `/mnt/git` | Pointing a second doorway at the same room |
| **DaemonSet** | One platform pod **per node**, always | A **building engineer on every floor** |
| **`CONFIG_PATH`** | Env var telling the app *which* path under `/mnt/git` to use (live trunk, tag, or SHA) | Which shelf in the library you agreed to read from |
| **`nsenter` (host mount namespace)** | Run mount work as if on the **node itself**, not only inside a container’s private view | Operating the real building HVAC, not a mock panel in a closet that the lobby never sees |

**How the pieces fit (one sentence):** the replica feeds each node; **FUSE** publishes a virtual Git tree on that node; **CSI** attaches that tree into each app pod; the app chooses a path via **`CONFIG_PATH`**.

